    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.goldberg_radzik - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.goldberg_radzik.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.goldberg_radzik()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute shortest paths from a source with the Goldberg-Radzik algorithm.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.goldberg_radzik()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute shortest paths from a source with the Goldberg-Radzik algorithm.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.goldberg_radzik()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Compute shortest paths from a source with the Goldberg-Radzik algorithm.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute shortest paths from a source with the Goldberg-Radzik algorithm.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L65027-L65161){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.goldberg_radzik(\n    source,\n    weighted=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Goldberg-Radzik shortest paths](../assets/graphs/goldberg-radzik.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Four nodes with directed weighted edges A to B 4, A to C 2, C to B −3 and B to D 1, searched from A (outlined). The shortest-path tree A, C, B, D is in the accent color and each node carries its distance as a badge: C 2, B 2 + (−3) = −1, D 0; the direct edge A to B stays faint. The table lists each node's predecessor and distance.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nLike Bellman-Ford, the Goldberg-Radzik algorithm finds shortest paths when some edge\nweights are negative (as long as no cycle reachable from `source` has negative total\nweight), but it usually needs far fewer passes over the edges. Each pass first sorts the\nnodes whose distance just improved, along with everything reachable from them through\nedges that would improve a distance, into topological order, and then relaxes their\nout-edges in that order, so an improvement travels down a whole chain of edges in a\nsingle pass.\n\nThe result is a shortest-path tree: each reached node's predecessor on one shortest path\nfrom `source`, and each node's distance. Use `predecessor()` to get every predecessor on\ntied shortest paths.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The start node, as an ID or label.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to use edge weights. If `None` (default), follows the graph's `weighted`\n    flag; edges without a weight count as `1.0`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [tuple[dict[int, int | None], dict[int, int | float]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A `(predecessors, distances)` pair, each keyed by the IDs of the nodes reachable from\n    `source`, in ID order. The source's predecessor is `None` and its distance is `0`.\n    Distances are `int` hops when unweighted.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `source` doesn't exist, or a cycle of negative total weight is reachable from it.\n    In an undirected graph, any reachable negative edge is such a cycle (there and back).\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nGoldberg and Radzik (1993) keep the $O(VE)$ worst case of Bellman-Ford but usually run\nmuch faster. A pass takes the set $B$ of nodes relabeled in the previous pass, and a\ndepth-first search from $B$ along edges of negative reduced cost\n$d(u) + w(u, v) - d(v) < 0$ collects the set $A$ to scan in topological order. A back\nedge found by this search closes a cycle containing a negative reduced-cost edge, which\nmust have negative total weight, so negative cycles are detected as they appear. The\npass then relaxes the out-edges of $A$ in order, and the nodes it improves form the next\n$B$. The search and the order of passes follow NetworkX's `goldberg_radzik`, whose\ndistances this method matches; when shortest paths tie, the predecessor picked may\ndiffer.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~bellman_ford_shortest_path`) \n\n:   A shortest path to one target, with negative weights.\n\n[](`~johnson`) \n\n:   Shortest paths between all pairs, with negative weights.\n\n[](`~find_negative_cycle`) \n\n:   The cycle that makes shortest paths undefined.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe direct edge from A to B costs `4`, but the route through C costs `2 + (-3) = -1`:\n\n::: {#2522912a .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\", 4.0), (\"A\", \"C\", 2.0), (\"C\", \"B\", -3.0), (\"B\", \"D\", 1.0)],\n    directed=True,\n)\n\npred, dist = graph.goldberg_radzik(\"A\")\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n{\n    label(n): (None if p is None else label(p), dist[n])\n    for n, p in pred.items()\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{'A': (None, 0.0), 'B': ('C', -1.0), 'C': ('A', 2.0), 'D': ('B', 0.0)}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Changing yen into every other currency\n\n`currency()` holds the exchange rates quoted between six currencies, each edge weighted by\nthe negative log of its rate, so a distance converts back to a rate with\n`math.exp(-distance)`. With `arbitrage=False`, no cycle has a negative total weight.\nFrom the yen, the best routes to the pound, the Swiss franc and the Canadian dollar all go\nthrough the US dollar:\n\n::: {#fe3dd52c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport math\nimport polars as pl\n\nfx = ec.currency(arbitrage=False)\npred, dist = fx.goldberg_radzik(\"JPY\")\nlabel = fx.get_label_from_id\n\npl.DataFrame({\n    \"currency\": [label(n) for n in pred],\n    \"previous\": [None if p is None else label(p) for p in pred.values()],\n    \"per_10000_yen\": [\n        round(10_000 * math.exp(-d), 2) for d in dist.values()\n    ],\n})\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (6, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>currency</th><th>previous</th><th>per_10000_yen</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;USD&quot;</td><td>&quot;JPY&quot;</td><td>66.6</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;EUR&quot;</td><td>&quot;JPY&quot;</td><td>61.64</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;GBP&quot;</td><td>&quot;USD&quot;</td><td>52.39</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;JPY&quot;</td><td>null</td><td>10000.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;CHF&quot;</td><td>&quot;USD&quot;</td><td>59.4</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;CAD&quot;</td><td>&quot;USD&quot;</td><td>91.14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe predecessors make a tree of best routes from the yen:\n\n::: {#ff268777 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntree = [(p, n) for n, p in pred.items() if p is not None]\nfx.plot(highlight=tree, label_by=\"label\", curved_edges=True, seed=4)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height